task.description:
The guard that keeps a "Blocks" edge from moving the open form's expectedHead is asserted by nothing. Deleting it leaves the whole internal/webui package passing (verified by the PR #29 reviewer).

Guard, internal/webui/assets/index.html:1703:

    function adoptDependencyHead(controller, dependentID, document) {
      if (dependentID !== controller.taskID) return;
      controller.adoptTaskHead(document.task && document.task.head || "");
    }

The test that is supposed to cover it is TestHandlerClientDetailFormAdoptsTheHeadItsOwnDependencyEditMoved at internal/webui/handler_test.go:5507, whose doc comment ends "A \"Blocks\" edge is stored on the other task and must not move it." The design doc claims the coverage at docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-06-optimistic-web-mutation-queue-design.md:301: "a dependency edge written to the open task moves the head the form proposes, and the mirrored direction does not."

Why it does not cover it: assertion ordering. Step 2 adds a Blocks edge, which without the guard would wrongly adopt blocked-head-2. Step 3 then removes a Depends-On edge and legitimately overwrites expectedHead with head-3. The test's single assertion is on the final request body (expectedHead "head-3"), which cannot observe the wrong adoption in step 2 because step 3 overwrote it either way.

The regression this is meant to catch is real and user-visible: a user adds a "Blocks" edge from task T's sidebar, then saves the title. Without the guard T's form proposes task B's head, and the save is refused as "That task changed elsewhere" for a change nobody made.

Fix is cheap: assert the head after the Blocks add and before the Depends-On remove — e.g. capture the request body of an intervening save, or assert the controller's expectedHead directly at that point. The design doc's Testing list should not claim coverage the suite does not have; WB-01KZBT3J1AMW4E5F688WPNZGJZ explicitly required that doc to match shipped behavior.

Deferred from PR #29 (merged as a2fd315) as a minor review finding: the shipped guard is correct, so this is missing regression protection and an inaccurate doc claim rather than a live defect.
